 content_en = E'## Why SQL is the analyst''s core skill\n\nSpreadsheets stop scaling around tens of thousands of rows. **SQL** talks directly to the database where the real data lives — millions of rows, always current. If you can describe the data you want in a sentence, SQL can fetch it.\n\nEvery query in this lesson runs against the **Playground → SQL** sandbox, so try them as you read.\n\n---\n\n## The TokoSegar database\n\nOur sandbox has four related tables. The **relationships** between them are what make SQL powerful:\n\n| Table | Key columns | Relates to |\n|-------|-------------|-----------|\n| `customers` | id, name, region, city, membership | — |\n| `products` | id, name, category, price, stock | — |\n| `orders` | id, **customer_id**, order_date, status, total_amount | customer_id → customers.id |\n| `order_items` | id, **order_id**, **product_id**, quantity, unit_price | order_id → orders.id, product_id → products.id |\n\n`customers → orders → order_items → products`\n\nA **primary key** uniquely identifies a row (`customers.id`). A **foreign key** points at another table''s primary key (`orders.customer_id`). Joins follow those links.\n\n---\n\n## 1. SELECT & WHERE\n\n```sql\nSELECT name, city, membership\nFROM customers\nWHERE membership = ''premium'';\n```\n\nCommon `WHERE` operators:\n\n| Operator | Meaning | Example |\n|----------|---------|---------|\n| `=` `<>` | equals / not equals | `status = ''completed''` |\n| `>` `<` `>=` `<=` | comparisons | `price > 1000000` |\n| `LIKE` | pattern match | `city LIKE ''Ja%''` (starts with Ja) |\n| `IN` | in a list | `region IN (''Java'',''Bali'')` |\n| `BETWEEN` | range | `order_date BETWEEN ''2024-01-01'' AND ''2024-03-31''` |\n\n---\n\n## 2. ORDER BY & LIMIT\n\n```sql\nSELECT name, price\nFROM products\nORDER BY price DESC\nLIMIT 3;\n```\nSorts most-expensive first and returns the top 3. `ASC` is the default; `DESC` reverses it.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Aggregation with GROUP BY\n\nAggregates collapse many rows into one summary row **per group**.\n\n```sql\nSELECT category, COUNT(*) AS n_products, AVG(price) AS avg_price\nFROM products\nGROUP BY category;\n```\n\n| category | n_products | avg_price |\n|----------|-----------|-----------|\n| Electronics | 5 | … |\n| Books | 3 | … |\n| Furniture | 2 | … |\n\n| Function | Returns |\n|----------|---------|\n| `COUNT(*)` | Number of rows |\n| `SUM(col)` | Total |\n| `AVG(col)` | Average |\n| `MIN`/`MAX` | Smallest / largest |\n\n---\n\n## 4. WHERE vs HAVING\n\nBoth filter — but at different stages.\n\n| Clause | Filters | Runs | Can use aggregates? |\n|--------|---------|------|---------------------|\n| `WHERE` | individual rows | **before** grouping | No |\n| `HAVING` | groups | **after** grouping | Yes |\n\n```sql\nSELECT region, COUNT(*) AS n_customers\nFROM customers\nWHERE membership = ''premium''   -- row filter, before grouping\nGROUP BY region\nHAVING COUNT(*) >= 2;            -- group filter, after grouping\n```\n\n---\n\n## 5. JOINs — combining tables\n\nMost real questions need more than one table. A **JOIN** stitches rows together on a matching key.\n\n### INNER JOIN (only matching rows)\n\n"Show each order with the customer''s name":\n\n```sql\nSELECT o.id AS order_id, c.name, o.total_amount\nFROM orders o\nJOIN customers c ON o.customer_id = c.id;\n```\n\n| order_id | name | total_amount |\n|----------|------|--------------|\n| 1 | Budi Santoso | 12785000 |\n| 2 | Siti Rahayu | 450000 |\n| … | … | … |\n\nThe `ON` clause is the link: `orders.customer_id = customers.id`. Table **aliases** (`o`, `c`) keep it short. An INNER JOIN keeps only rows that match on **both** sides.\n\n### LEFT JOIN (keep all left rows)\n\n"Every customer, even those with no orders":\n\n```sql\nSELECT c.name, COUNT(o.id) AS n_orders\nFROM customers c\nLEFT JOIN orders o ON o.customer_id = c.id\nGROUP BY c.name;\n```\nA `LEFT JOIN` keeps every `customers` row; customers with no order show `n_orders = 0` instead of vanishing. Use it when "the rows with no match still matter".\n\n### Multi-table JOIN\n\n"How many units of each product were sold?" needs three tables — `order_items` (the quantities) joined to `products` (the names):\n\n```sql\nSELECT p.name, SUM(oi.quantity) AS units_sold\nFROM order_items oi\nJOIN products p ON oi.product_id = p.id\nGROUP BY p.name\nORDER BY units_sold DESC;\n```\n\nChain as many joins as you need — each `JOIN … ON …` adds one more table along a foreign key.\n\n| Join type | Keeps |\n|-----------|-------|\n| `INNER JOIN` | Only rows matching in both tables |\n| `LEFT JOIN` | All left-table rows + matches (NULLs where none) |\n| `RIGHT JOIN` | All right-table rows + matches |\n\n---\n\n## 6. Building a query step by step\n\n```\nStart from the question → "revenue per region"\n↓\nFind the tables → orders (amount) + customers (region)\n↓\nJoin them → JOIN customers c ON o.customer_id = c.id\n↓\nGroup & aggregate → GROUP BY c.region, SUM(o.total_amount)\n↓\nFilter & sort → WHERE status = ''completed'', ORDER BY total DESC\n```\n\n> **Mini case study — revenue per region.** TokoSegar asked "which region earns most?" One query answered it:\n```sql\nSELECT c.region, SUM(o.total_amount) AS revenue\nFROM orders o\nJOIN customers c ON o.customer_id = c.id\nWHERE o.status = ''completed''\nGROUP BY c.region\nORDER BY revenue DESC;\n```\nJava dominated — but revenue *per customer* told a subtler story, which is exactly the kind of follow-up a good analyst asks next.\n\n---\n\n## Common pitfalls\n\n- **Forgetting the `ON` condition.** `FROM orders, customers` with no join key produces a *cross join* — every row paired with every row (750 rows instead of 50).\n- **`WHERE` on an aggregate.** `WHERE COUNT(*) > 2` errors — use `HAVING`.\n- **Ambiguous columns.** When two tables share a column name (`id`), always qualify it: `o.id`, `c.id`.\n- **INNER JOIN hiding rows.** If you expect every customer but some have no orders, INNER JOIN silently drops them — use LEFT JOIN.\n- **Selecting non-grouped columns.** Every non-aggregated column in `SELECT` must appear in `GROUP BY`.\n\n---\n\n## Glossary\n\n| Term | Meaning |\n|------|---------|\n| Primary key | Column that uniquely identifies a row |\n| Foreign key | Column pointing at another table''s primary key |\n| JOIN | Combines rows from two tables on a matching key |\n| INNER JOIN | Keeps only rows matching in both tables |\n| LEFT JOIN | Keeps all left rows, NULLs where no match |\n| Alias | A short name for a table or column (`orders o`) |\n| Aggregate | A function that summarises many rows (SUM, COUNT…) |\n\n---\n\n## Key Takeaways\n\n1. SQL fetches data straight from the database — describe what you want, and it returns it.\n2. `SELECT`/`WHERE` pick columns and rows; `ORDER BY`/`LIMIT` sort and cap; `GROUP BY` summarises.\n3. `WHERE` filters rows **before** grouping; `HAVING` filters groups **after** — and only `HAVING` can use aggregates.\n4. **JOINs** combine tables on a key: `INNER` keeps matches only, `LEFT` keeps all left rows. Chain joins across foreign keys for multi-table questions.\n5. Always qualify shared column names and give every join an `ON` condition.\n\n> 💡 Run every query above in **Playground → SQL**, then complete the SQL exercises below.',
 content_id = E'## Kenapa SQL adalah skill inti analyst\n\nSpreadsheet berhenti sanggup di kisaran puluhan ribu baris. **SQL** berbicara langsung ke database tempat data asli berada — jutaan baris, selalu terkini. Kalau Anda bisa mendeskripsikan data yang diinginkan dalam satu kalimat, SQL bisa mengambilnya.\n\nSetiap query di pelajaran ini berjalan di sandbox **Playground → SQL**, jadi cobalah sambil membaca.\n\n---\n\n## Database TokoSegar\n\nSandbox kita punya empat tabel yang saling terhubung. **Relasi** antar tabel inilah yang membuat SQL bertenaga:\n\n| Tabel | Kolom kunci | Terhubung ke |\n|-------|-------------|--------------|\n| `customers` | id, name, region, city, membership | — |\n| `products` | id, name, category, price, stock | — |\n| `orders` | id, **customer_id**, order_date, status, total_amount | customer_id → customers.id |\n| `order_items` | id, **order_id**, **product_id**, quantity, unit_price | order_id → orders.id, product_id → products.id |\n\n`customers → orders → order_items → products`\n\n**Primary key** mengidentifikasi baris secara unik (`customers.id`). **Foreign key** menunjuk ke primary key tabel lain (`orders.customer_id`). Join mengikuti tautan itu.\n\n---\n\n## 1. SELECT & WHERE\n\n```sql\nSELECT name, city, membership\nFROM customers\nWHERE membership = ''premium'';\n```\n\nOperator `WHERE` yang umum:\n\n| Operator | Arti | Contoh |\n|----------|------|--------|\n| `=` `<>` | sama / tidak sama | `status = ''completed''` |\n| `>` `<` `>=` `<=` | perbandingan | `price > 1000000` |\n| `LIKE` | pencocokan pola | `city LIKE ''Ja%''` (diawali Ja) |\n| `IN` | ada di daftar | `region IN (''Java'',''Bali'')` |\n| `BETWEEN` | rentang | `order_date BETWEEN ''2024-01-01'' AND ''2024-03-31''` |\n\n---\n\n## 2. ORDER BY & LIMIT\n\n```sql\nSELECT name, price\nFROM products\nORDER BY price DESC\nLIMIT 3;\n```\nMengurutkan termahal dulu dan mengembalikan 3 teratas. `ASC` adalah default; `DESC` membalikkannya.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Agregasi dengan GROUP BY\n\nAgregat memadatkan banyak baris menjadi satu baris ringkasan **per grup**.\n\n```sql\nSELECT category, COUNT(*) AS n_products, AVG(price) AS avg_price\nFROM products\nGROUP BY category;\n```\n\n| category | n_products | avg_price |\n|----------|-----------|-----------|\n| Electronics | 5 | … |\n| Books | 3 | … |\n| Furniture | 2 | … |\n\n| Fungsi | Mengembalikan |\n|--------|---------------|\n| `COUNT(*)` | Jumlah baris |\n| `SUM(col)` | Total |\n| `AVG(col)` | Rata-rata |\n| `MIN`/`MAX` | Terkecil / terbesar |\n\n---\n\n## 4. WHERE vs HAVING\n\nKeduanya menyaring — tapi di tahap berbeda.\n\n| Klausa | Menyaring | Berjalan | Bisa pakai agregat? |\n|--------|-----------|----------|---------------------|\n| `WHERE` | baris individual | **sebelum** grouping | Tidak |\n| `HAVING` | grup | **setelah** grouping | Ya |\n\n```sql\nSELECT region, COUNT(*) AS n_customers\nFROM customers\nWHERE membership = ''premium''   -- filter baris, sebelum grouping\nGROUP BY region\nHAVING COUNT(*) >= 2;            -- filter grup, setelah grouping\n```\n\n---\n\n## 5. JOIN — menggabungkan tabel\n\nSebagian besar pertanyaan nyata butuh lebih dari satu tabel. **JOIN** menjahit baris berdasarkan kunci yang cocok.\n\n### INNER JOIN (hanya baris yang cocok)\n\n"Tampilkan tiap order dengan nama pelanggannya":\n\n```sql\nSELECT o.id AS order_id, c.name, o.total_amount\nFROM orders o\nJOIN customers c ON o.customer_id = c.id;\n```\n\n| order_id | name | total_amount |\n|----------|------|--------------|\n| 1 | Budi Santoso | 12785000 |\n| 2 | Siti Rahayu | 450000 |\n| … | … | … |\n\nKlausa `ON` adalah tautannya: `orders.customer_id = customers.id`. **Alias** tabel (`o`, `c`) membuatnya ringkas. INNER JOIN hanya menyimpan baris yang cocok di **kedua** sisi.\n\n### LEFT JOIN (simpan semua baris kiri)\n\n"Setiap pelanggan, termasuk yang belum pernah order":\n\n```sql\nSELECT c.name, COUNT(o.id) AS n_orders\nFROM customers c\nLEFT JOIN orders o ON o.customer_id = c.id\nGROUP BY c.name;\n```\n`LEFT JOIN` menyimpan setiap baris `customers`; pelanggan tanpa order menampilkan `n_orders = 0` alih-alih menghilang. Gunakan saat "baris tanpa kecocokan tetap penting".\n\n### JOIN banyak tabel\n\n"Berapa unit tiap produk terjual?" butuh tiga tabel — `order_items` (kuantitasnya) di-join ke `products` (namanya):\n\n```sql\nSELECT p.name, SUM(oi.quantity) AS units_sold\nFROM order_items oi\nJOIN products p ON oi.product_id = p.id\nGROUP BY p.name\nORDER BY units_sold DESC;\n```\n\nRantai sebanyak yang diperlukan — tiap `JOIN … ON …` menambah satu tabel lagi mengikuti foreign key.\n\n| Tipe join | Menyimpan |\n|-----------|-----------|\n| `INNER JOIN` | Hanya baris yang cocok di kedua tabel |\n| `LEFT JOIN` | Semua baris tabel kiri + kecocokan (NULL bila tidak ada) |\n| `RIGHT JOIN` | Semua baris tabel kanan + kecocokan |\n\n---\n\n## 6. Menyusun query bertahap\n\n```\nMulai dari pertanyaan → "revenue per region"\n↓\nTemukan tabel → orders (amount) + customers (region)\n↓\nJoin → JOIN customers c ON o.customer_id = c.id\n↓\nGroup & agregasi → GROUP BY c.region, SUM(o.total_amount)\n↓\nFilter & urutkan → WHERE status = ''completed'', ORDER BY total DESC\n```\n\n> **Studi kasus mini — revenue per region.** TokoSegar bertanya "region mana yang paling menghasilkan?" Satu query menjawabnya:\n```sql\nSELECT c.region, SUM(o.total_amount) AS revenue\nFROM orders o\nJOIN customers c ON o.customer_id = c.id\nWHERE o.status = ''completed''\nGROUP BY c.region\nORDER BY revenue DESC;\n```\nJava mendominasi — tapi revenue *per pelanggan* menceritakan hal yang lebih halus, tepat jenis pertanyaan lanjutan yang diajukan analyst yang baik.\n\n---\n\n## Kesalahan umum\n\n- **Lupa kondisi `ON`.** `FROM orders, customers` tanpa kunci join menghasilkan *cross join* — tiap baris dipasangkan dengan tiap baris (750 baris, bukan 50).\n- **`WHERE` pada agregat.** `WHERE COUNT(*) > 2` error — gunakan `HAVING`.\n- **Kolom ambigu.** Saat dua tabel punya nama kolom sama (`id`), selalu kualifikasi: `o.id`, `c.id`.\n- **INNER JOIN menyembunyikan baris.** Kalau Anda mengharap semua pelanggan tapi sebagian tanpa order, INNER JOIN diam-diam membuangnya — pakai LEFT JOIN.\n- **Memilih kolom non-grup.** Setiap kolom non-agregat di `SELECT` harus muncul di `GROUP BY`.\n\n---\n\n## Glosarium\n\n| Istilah | Arti |\n|---------|------|\n| Primary key | Kolom yang mengidentifikasi baris secara unik |\n| Foreign key | Kolom yang menunjuk ke primary key tabel lain |\n| JOIN | Menggabungkan baris dua tabel berdasarkan kunci yang cocok |\n| INNER JOIN | Hanya menyimpan baris yang cocok di kedua tabel |\n| LEFT JOIN | Menyimpan semua baris kiri, NULL bila tak ada kecocokan |\n| Alias | Nama pendek untuk tabel/kolom (`orders o`) |\n| Agregat | Fungsi yang meringkas banyak baris (SUM, COUNT…) |\n\n---\n\n## Kesimpulan Utama\n\n1. SQL mengambil data langsung dari database — deskripsikan yang Anda mau, dan ia mengembalikannya.\n2. `SELECT`/`WHERE` memilih kolom dan baris; `ORDER BY`/`LIMIT` mengurutkan dan membatasi; `GROUP BY` meringkas.\n3. `WHERE` menyaring baris **sebelum** grouping; `HAVING` menyaring grup **setelah** — dan hanya `HAVING` yang bisa memakai agregat.\n4. **JOIN** menggabungkan tabel lewat kunci: `INNER` hanya kecocokan, `LEFT` menyimpan semua baris kiri. Rantai join lewat foreign key untuk pertanyaan banyak tabel.\n5. Selalu kualifikasi nama kolom yang dipakai bersama dan beri setiap join kondisi `ON`.\n\n> 💡 Jalankan setiap query di atas di **Playground → SQL**, lalu selesaikan exercise SQL di bawah.'
